menuconfig KFSW_GPREDICT
	bool "K-FSW pass tracking module"
	help
	  Enable pass tracking: the state machine that decides whether a gap in
	  the bearings is a predictor that stalled or a pass that ended. The
	  hamlib conversations with gpredict itself are not part of this module.

if KFSW_GPREDICT

config KFSW_GPREDICT_GRACE_MS
	int "Grace period before silence ends the pass, in milliseconds"
	range 250 600000
	default 5000
	help
	  How long the antenna holds its last bearing before parking. Too short
	  parks in the middle of a pass every time the predictor or the link to
	  it hesitates, and the rest of the pass is lost. Too long leaves the
	  antenna pointing at empty sky after the pass, and the next pass starts
	  with a full slew instead of a short one. Half the shortest useful pass
	  is a safe ceiling.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_PARK_AZIMUTH_DEG
	int "Park azimuth in degrees"
	range 0 360
	default 0
	help
	  Where the antenna waits between passes. A park bearing on the far side
	  of the travel from the usual rise azimuth spends the start of every
	  pass slewing.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_PARK_ELEVATION_DEG
	int "Park elevation in degrees"
	range 0 90
	default 0
	help
	  Low sheds wind, water and ice onto the mast instead of into the dish,
	  and keeps the sun out of the feed. High shortens the slew into the
	  next pass. Nothing between the two is free.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_AZIMUTH_MIN_DEG
	int "Lowest azimuth the rotator may be sent, in degrees"
	range 0 360
	default 0
	help
	  Narrower than the rotator can reach refuses bearings it would have
	  followed, and the refused ones are at the start and end of a pass where
	  the link is already worst. Wider than it can reach drives it into its
	  stops.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_AZIMUTH_MAX_DEG
	int "Highest azimuth the rotator may be sent, in degrees"
	range 0 360
	default 360
	help
	  See the minimum. The two together are the travel this module will
	  command, and a bearing outside them is refused rather than clamped: a
	  clamped bearing points at the wrong sky and says nothing about it.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_ELEVATION_MIN_DEG
	int "Lowest elevation the rotator may be sent, in degrees"
	range 0 90
	default 0
	help
	  Zero is the horizon, which is the floor whatever else is configured.
	  Raising it refuses the first and last minutes of every pass, which is
	  where a low-elevation link is acquired.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_ELEVATION_MAX_DEG
	int "Highest elevation the rotator may be sent, in degrees"
	range 0 90
	default 90
	help
	  Below the rotator's real limit refuses the middle of a high pass, which
	  is the strongest part of it. At or above the limit drives the mast into
	  its stop at the top of the pass.

config KFSW_GPREDICT_PROFILES
	int "Spacecraft profile slots"
	range 1 8
	default 3
	help
	  Every slot is 56 bytes of RAM on the reference targets, reserved at
	  build time whether or not a profile is ever defined in it. Eight slots
	  cost 448 bytes; one slot leaves the node able to follow a single
	  spacecraft and nothing else.

endif # KFSW_GPREDICT
